			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844290">
									

<blockquote>
<p>a mysterious benevolent entity/company is trying to help humanity regain contact with their dead loved ones</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm admittedly still skeptical about this, since not only does the entity focus on grandmas only for seemingly no reason, the requirement of antique radios doesn't feel conducive to helping humanity as a whole. Would make more narrative sense if, say, you had some anomalous tinkerer who missed his grandma messing with his own radio, which made anomalous broadcasts, which trigger manifestations in other such radios within a certain radius.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I still like to point out that some articles do have a similar setup as mine, which I took the inspiration from.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Those articles are not your article. Readers voting on your eventual draft are not obligated to look at other successful articles and upvote your work because those articles you did not write are successful. If they don't like your work, they'll downvote, regardless of whether something else has been similar and successful.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, I planned this as an initial setup for future articles of mine.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, focus on the piece at hand here. If the major plot point of your material is too heavily reliant on future articles, readers might feel like something is missing and the piece comes off more as sloppy rather than mysterious. Remember to keep in mind the readers' point of view&#8212;they don't automatically know what's being developed the way you as the author do. If they feel like something is missing, their immediate response is &quot;something is missing&quot; and not &quot;the author must be working on something amazing that hasn't been posted yet&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Yep.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm admittedly having trouble believing this, but if you're certain that the audience won't be skeptical, by all means continue.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Oh yeah, that's the right word I am looking for. A tale in a format of what you've said, my bad.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A supplement page is inherently not a tale; it's an offshoot/support page for an SCP article.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

